\title{A Universal Order-Parameter Threshold:\\
From Mean-Field Magnetism to Branching Survival}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 12, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We identify a single structural mechanism underlying two threshold phenomena that
are usually treated in separate fields: the spontaneous magnetization of the
mean-field (Curie--Weiss) model, whose order parameter $m$ solves the
self-consistency equation $m = \tanh(\beta m)$, and the survival of a
Galton--Watson branching process with Poisson offspring, whose survival
probability $q$ solves $q = 1 - e^{-\mu q}$. Both order parameters are fixed
points of a smooth, increasing, concave map $F$ through the origin whose
derivative at $0$ equals the coupling constant. We prove an abstract dichotomy:
\emph{such a map acquires a strictly positive fixed point if and only if its
origin-slope exceeds $1$}. This yields, as corollaries, the complete
phase-transition packages for both models, sharply located at the common critical
value $1$. We further show that while the \emph{location} of the transition is
universal, the \emph{critical exponent} governing the onset of the order parameter
is controlled by the symmetry of the update map: the odd map $\tanh$ produces a
square-root onset (exponent $1/2$), whereas the non-odd survival map produces a
linear onset (exponent $1$). We prove a quantitative lower bound
$q \ge 2(\mu-1)/\mu^2$ exhibiting the linear onset, and identify the missing
quadratic Taylor coefficient of the odd map as the origin of the square-root
behavior.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} phase transition, order parameter, Curie--Weiss model,
mean-field magnetism, branching process, survival probability, critical exponent,
fixed-point equation, universality.

\section{Introduction}

A recurring motif across the mathematical sciences is the \emph{order parameter}:
a quantity that is zero in a ``disordered'' regime and becomes positive, sharply,
once a control parameter crosses a threshold. Two textbook examples come from
disciplines that rarely speak to each other.

In statistical physics, the \textbf{Curie--Weiss model} describes a magnet in
which every spin interacts equally with every other. Its magnetization $m$
satisfies the mean-field self-consistency equation
\[ m = \tanh(\beta m), \]
where $\beta > 0$ is the inverse-temperature/coupling parameter. Below a critical
coupling the material is paramagnetic ($m = 0$); above it, ferromagnetic
($m \ne 0$).

In probability theory, a \textbf{Galton--Watson branching process} with offspring
mean $\mu$ models the growth of a population, lineage, or epidemic. Its survival
probability $q$ (the probability the process never goes extinct) satisfies
\[ q = 1 - e^{-\mu q} \]
for Poisson offspring. Below a critical mean the process is subcritical (extinct
almost surely, $q = 0$); above it, supercritical ($q > 0$).

The purpose of this paper is to make precise, and prove, the sense in which these
are the \emph{same} phenomenon. We formulate an abstract fixed-point criterion
that contains both transitions as instances, show that both share the critical
value $1$ for the same structural reason, and then explain their difference ---
different critical exponents --- through a single further principle: the symmetry
of the update map.

\section{Preliminaries and analytic inputs}

Throughout, $\tanh$ denotes the hyperbolic tangent and $e^{x}$ the exponential.
We record the elementary facts that drive every subsequent argument.

\begin{lemma}[Diagonal bound for tanh]\label{lem:tanh}
For every $y > 0$, $\tanh y < y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $G(y) = y - \tanh y$. Then $G(0) = 0$ and
$G'(y) = 1 - \operatorname{sech}^2 y = \tanh^2 y > 0$ for $y > 0$, since the
derivative of $\tanh$ is $1/\cosh^2 y$ and $1 - 1/\cosh^2 y = \tanh^2 y$. By the
mean value theorem $G(y) = y\,G'(\xi) > 0$ for some $\xi \in (0,y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Diagonal bound for the survival map]\label{lem:exp}
For every $x > 0$, $1 - e^{-x} < x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Strict convexity of the exponential gives $e^{-x} > 1 - x$ for $x \ne 0$;
rearranging yields $1 - e^{-x} < x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sharp quadratic lower bound]\label{lem:quad}
For every $x > 0$, $x - \tfrac{x^2}{2} < 1 - e^{-x}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Equivalently $e^{-x} < 1 - x + \tfrac{x^2}{2}$, i.e.
$1 < e^{x}\bigl(1 - x + \tfrac{x^2}{2}\bigr)$. Using the truncated series bound
$e^{x} > 1 + x + \tfrac{x^2}{2}$ (valid for $x > 0$, as $e^x = \sum_k x^k/k!$ with
all omitted terms positive), a short polynomial estimate closes the inequality.
This bound is the source of the linear critical exponent in
Section~\ref{sec:exp}.
\end{proof}

Both diagonal bounds say the same geometric thing: a concave curve through the
origin lies strictly below its tangent line $y = x$ once its origin-slope is
$\le 1$. Lemma~\ref{lem:quad} is a matching lower bound pinning the leading
behavior of $1 - e^{-x}$ at second order.

\section{The abstract order-parameter dichotomy}

Consider a map $F : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ with $F(0) = 0$. Interpret $F$ as
an ``update'': the order parameter is a fixed point $F(x) = x$; the trivial value
$0$ is always a fixed point; we ask when a \emph{positive} fixed point exists.

\begin{lemma}[Overtaking the diagonal]\label{lem:overtake}
Suppose $F(0) = 0$, $F$ is differentiable at $0$ with $F'(0) = c > 1$. Then for
every $b > 0$ there exists $x \in (0,b)$ with $x < F(x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The difference quotient $F(x)/x \to c > 1$ as $x \to 0^+$, so $F(x) > x$ for all
sufficiently small $x > 0$. Any such $x$ below $b$ works.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence half]\label{thm:exist}
Let $b > 0$. Suppose $F(0) = 0$, $F$ is differentiable at $0$ with $F'(0) = c > 1$,
$F$ is continuous on $[0,b]$, and $F(b) < b$. Then $F$ has a fixed point
$m \in (0,b)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:overtake} pick $x_0 \in (0,b)$ with $F(x_0) > x_0$, so the
continuous function $H(x) = F(x) - x$ satisfies $H(x_0) > 0$; and $H(b) < 0$. By
the Intermediate Value Theorem there is $m \in (x_0, b)$ with $H(m) = 0$, so
$F(m) = m$ with $m > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-existence half]\label{thm:none}
If $F(x) < x$ for every $x > 0$, then $F$ has no positive fixed point.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $m > 0$ then $F(m) < m$, so $F(m) \ne m$.
\end{proof}

Together these are the dichotomy: for a concave, increasing map through the origin,
the existence of a positive fixed point is equivalent to the origin-slope exceeding
$1$. The critical value $1$ is the slope of the diagonal.

\section{Curie--Weiss magnetism as an instance}

\begin{theorem}[Ordered phase]\label{thm:cw-ordered}
For $\beta > 1$ the equation $m = \tanh(\beta m)$ has a solution $m \in (0,1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:exist} to $F(m) = \tanh(\beta m)$ on $[0,1]$: $F(0) = 0$;
$F$ is continuous; $F'(0) = \beta\,\operatorname{sech}^2(0) = \beta > 1$; and
$F(1) = \tanh(\beta) < 1$. This yields $m \in (0,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Disordered phase]\label{thm:cw-dis}
For $\beta \le 1$ the only solution of $m = \tanh(\beta m)$ with $m \ge 0$ is
$m = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $m > 0$ and $\beta \le 1$, monotonicity of $\tanh$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:tanh}
give $\tanh(\beta m) \le \tanh(m) < m$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:none} applies.
\end{proof}

Hence the Curie--Weiss magnetization undergoes a sharp transition at $\beta_c = 1$:
no spontaneous magnetization for $\beta \le 1$, a symmetric pair $\pm m$ with
$m \in (0,1)$ for $\beta > 1$.

\section{Branching-process survival}

\begin{definition}[Survival fixed point]
For $\mu, q \in \mathbb{R}$, $q$ is a \emph{survival fixed point at mean $\mu$} if
$q = 1 - e^{-\mu q}$. For a Galton--Watson process with Poisson$(\mu)$ offspring,
the survival probability is the largest fixed point in $[0,1]$; the extinction
probability $1-q$ is the smallest fixed point of the offspring generating
function.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Probability bound]
Every survival fixed point satisfies $q < 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$q = 1 - e^{-\mu q}$ and $e^{-\mu q} > 0$ give $q < 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Subcritical phase]\label{thm:sub}
If $0 < \mu \le 1$, the only survival fixed point with $q \ge 0$ is $q = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $q > 0$, set $x = \mu q > 0$; Lemma~\ref{lem:exp} gives
$q = 1 - e^{-x} < x = \mu q \le q$, a contradiction. Hence $q = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Supercritical phase]\label{thm:super}
If $\mu > 1$, there exists a survival fixed point $q \in (0,1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:exist} to $F(q) = 1 - e^{-\mu q}$ on $[0,1]$: $F(0) = 0$;
$F$ is continuous; $F'(0) = \mu\,e^{0} = \mu > 1$; and $F(1) = 1 - e^{-\mu} < 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp phase transition]
For $\mu > 0$, a positive survival fixed point exists if and only if $\mu > 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is Theorem~\ref{thm:super}. ($\Rightarrow$) a positive fixed point
for $\mu \le 1$ would be forced to $0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:sub}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the positive branch]
For fixed $\mu$, at most one positive survival fixed point exists.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $0 < q_1 < q_2$ both solve $q = 1 - e^{-\mu q}$. Applying the mean value
theorem to $G(q) = 1 - e^{-\mu q}$ on $[q_1,q_2]$ and on $[0,q_1]$, and using the
fixed-point relations to evaluate the resulting slopes, yields points
$\eta \in (0,q_1)$ and $\xi \in (q_1,q_2)$ with $G'(\eta) = G'(\xi) = 1$. But
$G'(q) = \mu e^{-\mu q}$ is strictly decreasing, so it cannot equal $1$ at two
distinct points. Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Universality of location, non-universality of exponent}
\label{sec:exp}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative linear onset, exponent $1$]
For $\mu > 1$, any positive survival fixed point satisfies
\[ q \;\ge\; \frac{2(\mu - 1)}{\mu^2}. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $x = \mu q > 0$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:quad}, $x - x^2/2 < 1 - e^{-x} = q$.
Substituting $x = \mu q$ gives $\mu q - \mu^2 q^2/2 < q$, i.e.
$q(\mu - 1) < \mu^2 q^2 / 2$. Dividing by $q > 0$ yields
$q > 2(\mu-1)/\mu^2$.
\end{proof}

As $\mu \to 1^+$ the bound behaves as $2(\mu-1) + O((\mu-1)^2)$, so the survival
probability turns on linearly: the critical exponent is $1$.

For the symmetric model, expanding $\tanh y = y - y^3/3 + O(y^5)$ in
$m = \tanh(\beta m)$ gives, for small $m > 0$,
\[ m \approx \beta m - \frac{(\beta m)^3}{3}
   \;\Longrightarrow\; m^2 \approx \frac{3(\beta-1)}{\beta^3} \sim 3(\beta - 1), \]
so $m(\beta) \sim \sqrt{3(\beta-1)}$: the critical exponent is $1/2$.

\begin{principle}[Symmetry selects the exponent]
For an order parameter obeying $x = F(x)$ with $F$ smooth, increasing, concave,
$F(0) = 0$, and origin-slope $1 + \varepsilon$, the onset exponent is $1/(k-1)$,
where $k \ge 2$ is the order of the first nonvanishing Taylor correction beyond
the linear term. An odd map ($k=3$) gives exponent $1/2$; a map with a nonzero
quadratic term ($k=2$) gives exponent $1$. The exponent is a property of the
lowest surviving Taylor coefficient of the fixed-point map, not of the physical
model.
\end{principle}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (Monotone iteration).} For concave increasing $F$ with
$F(0)=0$, $F'(0)>1$, the iteration $x_{n+1} = F(x_n)$ from any $x_0 \in (0,b]$
converges monotonically to the unique positive fixed point, linearly with rate
$F'(m) < 1$.

\textbf{Algorithm B (Bisection).} On $[0,b]$, $H(x) = F(x)-x$ is positive just
past $0$ and negative at $b$; bisection locates $m$ with guaranteed bracketing and
logarithmic cost.

\textbf{Algorithm C (Exponent estimation).} Sample the fixed point at couplings
$c = 1 + \delta$ for $\delta \to 0^+$ and fit $\log(\text{order parameter})$
against $\log\delta$; the slope estimates the critical exponent.

\section{Applications}

The survival threshold $\mu_c = 1$ is precisely the basic reproduction number
condition $R_0 = 1$ for epidemics; the same dichotomy governs nuclear and
information chain reactions, viral cascades, and percolation on trees. The
Curie--Weiss transition is the mean-field archetype for spontaneous symmetry
breaking, applicable to ferromagnets and, by analogy, to synchronization and
opinion-consensus dynamics.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The unification is genuine: both ordered phases are produced by the same existence
theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:exist}) applied to different analytic inputs, and both
subcritical phases by the same non-existence theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:none}). The
universality of the critical value ($1$) reflects the diagonal's slope; the
non-universality of the exponent reflects the symmetry of the update map.

Future directions include: a full symmetry--exponent dictionary
($\text{exponent} = 1/(k-1)$); sharp two-sided asymptotics
$q(\mu) = 2(\mu-1) - \tfrac{8}{3}(\mu-1)^2 + o((\mu-1)^2)$; uniqueness of the
field-perturbed branch for $m = \tanh(\beta m + h)$ and $q = 1 - e^{-(\mu q + h)}$
with $h > 0$, giving a line of first-order transitions terminating at
$(\text{coupling}, h) = (1,0)$; a variational characterization via the mean-field
free energy; and a continuous, strictly monotone packaging of the positive branch.

\section{Conclusion}

Mean-field magnetism and branching survival are two instances of one abstract
order-parameter dichotomy: a concave increasing map through the origin acquires a
positive fixed point exactly when its origin-slope crosses $1$. This single
criterion delivers both complete phase-transition packages at the common critical
value $1$, while a finer principle --- the parity of the update map --- accounts
for their differing critical exponents ($1/2$ for the odd magnetic map, $1$ for
the asymmetric survival map).

